-- Require trusted HTTPS for the tutorial without changing applied migration checksums.
UPDATE recipe
SET content = $recipe$Set up a public WordPress blog over trusted HTTPS, ready for scheduled news posts.

Required secrets: `WORDPRESS_ADMIN_USER`, `WORDPRESS_ADMIN_PASSWORD` and `WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD`. The guided tutorial collects the first two and generates the database password. Outside the tutorial, ask the user to add any missing values in workspace Settings → Secrets and restart. Check that each environment variable is set without printing it. Never paste a credential into chat, a command literal, an instruction file, or an outbox download.

Applying this recipe authorizes publishing this blog on ports 80 and 443 of this machine's public IP address. Use `https://$WORKSPACE_IP` (or the public IPv4 from `firewall list` if unset), never the platform hostname. Obtain a publicly trusted Let's Encrypt IP-address certificate; a domain is not required. Do not substitute plain HTTP, a self-signed certificate, or instructions to bypass a browser warning if issuance fails. Choose a site title and tagline without further questions.

1. In `~/project/blog`, create a Docker Compose project with the official `wordpress:apache`, `mariadb:lts`, `wordpress:cli`, `nginx:alpine` and `certbot/certbot` images. Use Certbot 5.4 or newer and verify its version supports `--ip-address` and the webroot plugin. Keep WordPress data, database data, ACME challenge files, and the complete `/etc/letsencrypt` tree in separate named Docker volumes. A bind mount from the harness is not necessarily a path on the Docker daemon's host. Persist proxy configuration in a named volume too, populated using a temporary container and standard input rather than a harness bind mount. Set `restart: unless-stopped` on the web, database and proxy services. Publish only the proxy on `80:80` and `443:443`; keep WordPress, database and CLI internal.
2. Use database name/user `wordpress`. Reference the `WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD` environment variable using required-value interpolation in Compose for the database user's password, database root password, and web and CLI `WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD`. Give web and CLI the same database host, name, user and password. Keep only environment variable references in Compose, never expanded values or a secrets `.env`. Do not run `docker compose config`, print container environments, enable shell tracing, or display commands with expanded credentials.
3. Start the database and web services, waiting for the database to be healthy and WordPress files to be initialized. Give the CLI the same WordPress volume, working directory `/var/www/html`, network and database environment as the web service, with a compatible UID/GID (33:33 for the Apache image). Set its entrypoint explicitly to `wp` and run it only on demand. Initially run the proxy with an HTTP-only configuration that serves `/.well-known/acme-challenge/` from the challenge volume and returns 404 for every other request. Open TCP ports 80 and 443 with `firewall open 80` and `firewall open 443`. Verify a test challenge file is reachable at the public IP before requesting a certificate; do not expose the WordPress setup screen over HTTP.
4. Mount the challenge volume in the on-demand Certbot service at `/var/www/acme`, and the certificate volume at `/etc/letsencrypt`. Request a production certificate using `docker compose run --rm certbot certonly --webroot --webroot-path /var/www/acme --ip-address "$WORKSPACE_IP" --preferred-profile shortlived --cert-name wordpress-ip --non-interactive --agree-tos --register-unsafely-without-email`. Reuse a valid existing certificate on retries; do not repeatedly force issuance. Keep the private key in the certificate volume, never in chat, downloads or instruction files. Certbot obtains IP certificates but does not install the proxy configuration for you.
5. Mount the complete certificate volume read-only in nginx. Configure port 443 with `/etc/letsencrypt/live/wordpress-ip/fullchain.pem` and `privkey.pem`, proxying to the internal WordPress service. Overwrite `Host`, `X-Forwarded-Proto` and other forwarding headers appropriately, and configure WordPress to recognize HTTPS from this internal proxy so it does not loop or generate HTTP links. On port 80 keep the ACME challenge location accessible and redirect every other request to the matching HTTPS IP URL. Validate nginx configuration before reloading it. Never redirect the ACME challenge to WordPress.
6. Check `wp core is-installed` before installing, so retries keep content. Install with site URL `https://$WORKSPACE_IP`, the admin username and password from the secrets, local placeholder email `admin@example.invalid` and `--skip-email`. Pass the password through standard input with `--prompt=admin_password`, not a command argument or file. For an existing installation, update `home` and `siteurl` and replace old HTTP site URLs safely, skipping GUID columns. Force HTTPS for admin access. Write an original welcome post and remove the default sample post and page only if present.
7. IP certificates use Let's Encrypt's shortlived profile and last about six days. Add a persistent workspace service script at `~/.config/workspaced/services/wordpress-tls-renew.sh`, using the platform's service runner, that runs `docker compose run --rm certbot renew --quiet` in `~/project/blog` every 12 hours and reloads nginx only after a successful renewal check. The script must remain in the foreground (for example an `exec bash` loop), survive workspace restarts, and log failures for diagnosis. Do not use an AI scheduled task, host cron, systemd or a Docker socket mount in the proxy. Check renewal configuration with `certbot renew --dry-run` and inspect the renewal service log. This renewal continues while the workspace runs; the four-hour demo still deletes everything on expiry.
8. Verify the public home page, first post and admin link using normal certificate validation, for example `curl --fail --location https://"$WORKSPACE_IP"/` without `-k` or `--insecure`. Check HTTP redirects to HTTPS, the certificate covers the public IP, and pages and assets use HTTPS without mixed content. If issuance, renewal or checks fail, fix the problem and repeat the checks. Do not claim a working HTTPS deployment until they pass.
9. Add `blog.md` under `~/.config/workspaced/instructions/`, so it appears in the user's Settings → Instructions and is shared by later sessions. Record the Compose directory, volume and service names, HTTPS URL, renewal service, and secret names only. Include a short "Publish a new article with WP-CLI" guide with working shell examples: change into `~/project/blog`; list published posts with `docker compose run --rm cli post list --post_status=publish --fields=ID,post_title,post_date --format=table`; write an article body to a local HTML file and stream it with `cat article.html | docker compose run --rm -T cli post create - --post_title='Article title' --post_status=publish --post_author=AUTHOR_ID --porcelain`; then verify the returned post ID and retrieve its permalink. Replace AUTHOR_ID with the actual numeric admin user ID and adjust commands to the CLI service you tested. Explain how to save a draft instead, publish it later with `post update`, and include the source URL and date for news articles. Use these tested commands for the welcome article rather than leaving untested examples. Explain how later scheduled sessions can read a current technology RSS feed, avoid duplicate source URLs, and publish an original summary with the source link and date, preserving HTTPS. Do not create the news task yet; the tutorial does that next.
10. Reply with clickable links to `https://$WORKSPACE_IP` and `https://$WORKSPACE_IP/wp-admin`, only after verifying them. Tell the user to sign in with the credentials they saved as secrets and find the CLI publishing guide under Settings → Instructions → blog. Do not reveal or export a credential or private key.

References: https://hub.docker.com/_/wordpress, https://developer.wordpress.org/cli/commands/core/install/, https://developer.wordpress.org/cli/commands/post/create/ and https://letsencrypt.org/2026/03/11/shorter-certs-certbot/
$recipe$
WHERE id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000b001';
